Subject: Validate the locale archive header and records before use
Origin: Filnix
Upstream-Status: Not submitted

_nl_load_locale_from_archive trusts the offsets, lengths and counts
stored in the locale-archive file: it maps the file, reads the locarhead
fields directly, and follows name_offset, locrec_offset and every
record offset into the mapping without checking them against the
mapping's length.  A truncated or inconsistent archive then reads
past the mapping, which Fil-C stops with a safety trap, or never
terminates: the hash probe loop stops only on an empty slot, and a
full hash table with no matching entry has none.  The 32-bit sums
wrap, too: a record with offset 0xffffff00 and len 0x100 passes the
check that is meant to bound it.

The loader now checks that the mapping holds the whole archive header,
that the head tables fit in it (summed in 64 bits, and narrowed to
size_t only after the page round-up), that the probe loop probes at
most namehash_size slots, that a name_offset lies inside the string
table and its name is NUL-terminated there, that a locrec_offset
holds the whole struct locrecent and is aligned for the direct field
accesses, and that offset + len stays inside the mapping as a 64-bit
sum.  Every failure takes the existing close_and_out path, so a
corrupt archive reads as a clean "locale not found".

The regression check is the locarch check of
nanofil/survey/glibc/regress.nix (regress/locarch.c), which writes
truncated, inconsistent and non-terminating archives and locale files
and expects setlocale to fail cleanly instead of trapping or hanging.

